# Browser verification must be bounded

Browser / computer-use checks have no timeout setting. A blocking browser check
that gets stuck — dead dev server, blank page, retry loop — stalls the whole
turn until the user cancels it. Assume that will happen and design around it.

## Default: don't

Skip browser verification for logic, data, backend, and refactor work, and for
small edits to components that already render. `npx tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, and
a bounded `curl` are cheaper and stronger signals.

Reach for a browser only when the deliverable is visual or time-based — new page
layouts, animation, crossfades, responsive behaviour — and say why.

## Never hand a browser a URL you have not proved is live

This is the most common way these hang. Before launching any browser check:

1. Start the dev server yourself in a background shell. Never ask the browser
   agent to start it, and never ask it to "wait for the server to come up".
2. Prove the exact URL responds, with a time-boxed request:

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -m 10 -w "%{http_code}\n" http://localhost:3000/the/path
```

3. If that does not return 2xx/3xx, stop. Report that the server is not serving
   and why. Do not launch a browser agent against a dead URL.

## Run it non-blocking

Launch browser checks with `run_in_background: true`. That is the only mechanism
that actually guarantees a stuck check cannot stall the turn.

## One pass, finite checklist

Give the check a numbered list of steps and tell it to report after those steps
even if something is ambiguous, rather than retrying. Do not launch a second
browser check to re-test the same hypothesis: these agents produce false
negatives (keystrokes landing in devtools, cached CSS, stale tabs), and chasing
them costs more than the verification is worth.

## If it stalls anyway

Say plainly that the change is unverified and why, then hand over the exact
steps to check it by hand. An honest "unverified" beats a hung turn.

## Deployed previews beat local browsers

When the branch is pushed and the host builds a deploy preview, verify against
that URL instead of a local dev server. It removes the whole class of "the
server never came up" hangs.
